 The probability of getting heads in a single flip of a fair coin is \(\frac{1}{2}\). Therefore, the probability of getting heads in exactly \(k\) out of 10 flips is given by the binomial probability formula: \[
P(X = k) = \binom{10}{k} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^k \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{10-k} = \binom{10}{k} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{10}
\] where \(\binom{10}{k}\) is the binomial coefficient, which represents the number of ways to choose \(k\) heads out of 10 flips. To find the probability of getting heads in at least 8 out of 10 flips, we need to sum the probabilities of getting exactly 8, 9, and 10 heads. The probability of getting exactly 8 heads is: \[
P(X = 8) = \binom{10}{8} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{10} = \frac{45}{1024}
\] The probability of getting exactly 9 heads is: \[
P(X = 9) = \binom{10}{9} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{10} = \frac{10}{1024}
\] The probability of getting exactly 10 heads is: \[
P(X = 10) = \binom{10}{10} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{10} = \frac{1}{1024}
\] So, the probability of getting at least 8 heads is: \[
P(X \geq 8) = P(X = 8) + P(X = 9) + P(X = 10) = \frac{45}{1024} + \frac{10}{1024} + \frac{1}{1024} = \frac{56}{1024} = \frac{7}{128}
\] The final answer is \(\boxed{\frac{7}{128}}\). </think> <answer> \(\frac{7}{128}\)</answer>

